  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14096769/assets/f164da846baf0488c472fbda94d10026247a6b9b613cc68d33d4ec43a5632d53.png)\n\n**From top to bottom**, the **exact** values of the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above are{{field-1}}\nand\n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(1,-1)$ and passes through the point $(-1,-3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(1,-1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y+1)^{2}=4p(x-1).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-1,-3),$ we substitute $x=-1$ and $y=-3$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3+1)^{2} & =4p(-1-1) \\\\ 4 & =4p(-2) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =-2(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =-2(0-1) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{2} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are $\\sqrt{2}-1$ and  $-\\sqrt{2}-1.$",
